  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Plutarch and Rhetoric shows early evidence of reach through open access availability and sustained use, with 981 recorded downloads and a Quality Education SDG alignment. The book also sits within a funded scholarly publishing context at KU Leuven, while the citation record is still at zero, suggesting that its longer-term scholarly uptake is still developing.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal for Plutarch and Rhetoric is practical readership: the book has been downloaded 981 times, with activity concentrated across the first months in the available record. That pattern suggests active consultation rather than isolated access, and it is consistent with a work that is being discovered through open dissemination.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book is openly available through an open-access route, which likely supports this early use. Its SDG alignment with Quality Education also points to relevance for teaching and learning contexts, especially in the humanities where books often serve as reference points for graduate study, seminar discussion, and independent research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly citation impact has not yet emerged in the record provided: OpenAlex reports 0 citations. That does not diminish the book\u2019s intellectual significance, but it does mean that any assessment of research influence should remain cautious and focused on current evidence rather than anticipated uptake.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication also carries signs of formal scholarly support, including funding from KU Leuven. Taken together, the record shows a book that is already being used and circulated, with its broader citation footprint still in an early stage. In a field such as classical studies and rhetoric, that combination often indicates a work positioned for slower, cumulative influence as it is absorbed into future scholarship and teaching.</p>",
